# Target C-II — certified `0.00122` terminal FREE surplus gap

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only numerical sharpening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II terminal FREE survivor sequences with `pi D-q=o(D)`  
**Refines:** `target_c_free_terminal_outer_energy_surplus_gap_2026-09-18.md`  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_outer_energy_surplus_gap_00122_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The outer-energy surplus-gap argument has enough exact margin to replace the safe
`0.0012` constant by

\[
\boxed{\varepsilon_1=0.00122=\frac{61}{50000}.}
\]

Thus every terminal full-core Type-II FREE survivor sequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf \mu_D
\ge
\mu_*+0.00122
=
1.954482849540479\ldots,
}
\tag{R.1}
\]

where

\[
\mu_*=
\frac\pi2+\frac25\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}+\frac{0.007707}{5}
=1.953262849540479\ldots .
\]

This uses exactly the same proof as the preceding note. Only four scalar cutoffs are
tightened. The new value is close to the numerical ceiling of this composition
(`~0.00122124`), so further decimal polishing of the same inequalities is not a useful
closure route.

---

## 1. Stability window

Assume for contradiction that along a terminal FREE subsequence

\[
\limsup\mu_D\le\mu_*+\varepsilon_1.
\]

The surplus-endpoint stability decomposition gives

\[
a\le5\varepsilon_1=0.0061,
\qquad
\delta\le\frac52\varepsilon_1=0.00305,
\tag{R.2}
\]

where

\[
a=\lim\left(\frac{\tau_D}{D}-\left(\frac\pi2+g\right)\right),
\qquad
\delta=\lim\frac{b-\operatorname{per}(F)}D,
\qquad g=0.007707.
\]

The all-train scalar therefore satisfies

\[
C=2s_2+g+a
\le2s_2+g+0.0061,
\qquad
s_2=\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}.
\tag{R.3}
\]

As in the preceding proof, if `R` is the atomic train mass outside a dominant exact
`pi/3` orbit, then

\[
R\le C-\frac{4\Delta_2}{C},
\qquad
\Delta_2=\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}.
\]

The exact certificate gives

\[
\boxed{R<0.027515.}
\tag{R.4}
\]

---

## 2. Dominant-orbit outer occurrence cutoff

Use

\[
z_1:=0.8705=\frac{1741}{2000}.
\]

For the hostile-audited dominant-orbit route-energy function

\[
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6),
\]

the alternating sine estimate gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
F(z_1)
&\ge
\frac{z_1^3}{216}-\frac{z_1^5}{155520}\\
&=0.00305067018440117\ldots\\
&>0.00305.
\end{aligned}
\tag{R.5}
\]

Since the entire outer detour is at most `0.00305D`, the dominant-orbit outer
occurrence density `K` obeys

\[
K\le\delta+z_1.
\tag{R.6}
\]

Residual atomic trains contribute at most `2R` further outer occurrences.

---

## 3. Forced O--O pair mass

The exact B--B/B--O/O--O endpoint transport identities from the preceding proof then
give

\[
\ell:=\lim\frac{L_O}{D}
\ge
\frac\pi2-g-a-z_1-2R.
\tag{R.7}
\]

Retaining O--O endpoint weight under loaded-hull side-length capacity loses at most
`delta` pair mass. Hence the retained O--O pair density `p` satisfies

\[
\begin{aligned}
p
&\ge
\frac\pi2-g-a-z_1-2R-\delta\\
&>0.628409326794896\ldots,
\end{aligned}
\tag{R.8}
\]

and in particular

\[
\boxed{p>0.6284.}
\tag{R.9}
\]

One of the three half-open projective sectors of width `pi/3` therefore contains
retained pair mass at least

\[
\boxed{
m_1=\frac{0.6284}{3}=0.209466666666666\ldots .}
\tag{R.10}
\]

---

## 4. One sector still exceeds the whole detour budget

The hostile-audited same-sector noncrossing O--O energy theorem gives

\[
E(m)=2m-2\sin m.
\]

For `m_1<pi/6`, the alternating sine estimate yields

\[
\begin{aligned}
E(m_1)
&\ge
\frac{m_1^3}{3}-\frac{m_1^5}{60}\\
&=0.00305681884583903\ldots\\
&>0.00305.
\end{aligned}
\tag{R.11}
\]

This selected terminal-edge energy is a sub-sum of the total outer route directional
energy, whose normalized total is `delta<=0.00305`. Contradiction.

Therefore (R.1) holds.

---

## 5. Saturation of this scalar composition

If one replaces the rational thresholds above by the exact inverse of `F` and uses the
exact residual expression

\[
R(C)=C-\frac{4\Delta_2}{C},
\]

the final sector-energy margin changes sign numerically near

\[
\varepsilon\approx0.00122124.
\]

That number is exploratory only. The certified theorem stops at `0.00122`, leaving an
exact positive margin in both load-bearing Taylor inequalities.

Accordingly, the next useful work is not another decimal of this endpoint gap. The
new information should be propagated into the terminal source-level ledgers. In
particular, canonical loaded-contact localization gives asymptotically

\[
\mu_D
=
\frac{q-k+m_B}{D}+o(1)
=
t_D+\frac{m_B}{D}+o(1),
\]

so (R.1) already forces the Pareto constraint

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\left(t_D+\frac{m_B}{D}\right)
\ge1.954482849540479\ldots .
}
\tag{R.12}
\]

Thus in the subbranch `m_B=o(D)` the terminal `t` floor itself improves by `0.00122`,
while a survivor remaining near the old `t` floor must carry a linear population of
nonunit force-hull sides. That is the branch split to combine next with the aggregate
Sections 121--123 detour/payment system.

No theorem-state file is changed.